From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: "D.R.racer" <drracer@seznam.cz>
Date: Tue, 20 Oct 2026 17:48:02 +0200
Subject: drm/sun4i: frontend: fix the source offset, filters and throughput
 of video planes

Three fixes for planes that go through the A10/A20 display frontend
(scaled or YUV planes, e.g. a VDPAU or Xv video window). They are one
patch because each builds on the code of the one before.

1. Honour the source offset of tiled semi-planar buffers

For DRM_FORMAT_MOD_ALLWINNER_TILED the start address was the linear
offset from drm_fb_dma_get_gem_addr() and only the end-tile column (X1)
was programmed, so any source not starting at (0,0) - e.g. a video plane
moved partly off-screen - was read from the wrong tiles. Program the
start address and the TB_OFF X0/Y0/X1 in-tile offsets per plane as the
Allwinner display driver (sunxi-3.4 de_fe.c, DISP_MOD_MB_UV_COMBINED)
does. Three-plane tiled formats (16x16 tiles there) keep the old code.

2. Select the scaler filter by the scaling ratio

The frontend loads one fixed set of scaler filters, which do not
low-pass when downscaling: any downscaled plane aliases, horizontally
and vertically.

The A10/A20 BSP picks, for each channel and direction, one of 15 filter
sets by the scaling ratio, with a stronger low-pass the more it
downscales; the first of them is the fixed set. Do the same on the A10
and A20, with the BSP's table and its default smoothing, and reload the
filters (with COEF_RDY) whenever a set changes. The A23/A33 keep the
fixed set.

The table is fir_tab_sun4i from drivers/video/sunxi/disp/de_be.c of
the linux-sunxi 3.4 kernel (Copyright (C) 2007-2012 Allwinner
Technology Co., Ltd., GPL-2.0-or-later), sets 1 to 15 unchanged.

3. Skip input lines to stay within the frontend's throughput

The A10/A20 frontend reads its source at a limited rate. When a plane
needs more than that per output line time, the plane comes out taller
and the backend slips lines on the whole screen. Measured on an A20
(DRAM at 432 MHz, a single 1080p60 HDMI head, 1920 pixel wide sources,
14.8 us line time):

- tiled NV12 (1.5 bytes/pixel) works up to 2x vertical downscaling
  (259 Mpixels/s) and breaks at 2.4x (311 Mpixels/s), a pixel rate
  limit near the 297 MHz frontend clock;
- linear XRGB8888 (4 bytes/pixel) works at 1.67x (0.87 GB/s) and
  breaks at 1.82x (0.94 GB/s), a DRAM read bandwidth limit.

Skip every other source line when a plane would exceed 260 Mpixels/s or
0.8 GB/s for the line time of the CRTC's mode, and reject planes that
exceed them even so in atomic_check instead of corrupting the screen.
The budgets keep a margin over the measurements, so some measured-good
planes are now refused (e.g. XRGB8888 downscaled by 3.33, 0.86 GB/s
after skipping), but they do not account for a slower DRAM clock
(Armbian's default is 384 MHz) or for the scanout of a second head.
Only progressive output modes were measured.

The lines are skipped by fetching the top field of an interlaced input
(INPUT_FMT scan mode, FIELD_CTRL 0), with the frame height as input
size and the vertical factor and filter computed from the halved line
count; this is the BSP's path for interlaced input (de_fe.c), which it
also uses for tiled video. It works for linear and tiled buffers alike,
which doubling the line stride would not: the tiled (MB32) layout steps
through the rows inside a tile in hardware. The first source line is
rounded down to a multiple of 2 * vsub so that luma and chroma start on
the same field line (this moves the crop up by at most 2 * vsub - 1
lines). 3D_CTRL is cleared, as the BSP does, so that no 3D input mode
can interfere.

Skipped lines are dropped without a pre-filter, so a plane that just
needs skipping aliases somewhat more than one that just does not.

Tested-on: Cubietruck (A20), 1080p60 HDMI, VDPAU tiled NV12 via the frontend, 1280x720 down to 480x270 (1.5x-4x): correct geometry, no aliasing; 320x180 (6x) refused as intended.
Tested-on: Cubietruck (A20), 1080p60 HDMI, DRAM 432 MHz: XR24 1920x1080 overlay scaled 0.75..0.333 (incl. 0.55/0.5, which pumped with the fixed 2x rule) stable, 0.30..0.25 refused; VDPAU tiled NV12 1280x720..480x270 correct, 384x216 and 320x180 refused.
Assisted-by: Claude:claude-opus-5-5
